1. Build auto-apply parser for LLM output (Python)
    [ ] 'harness/parser.py': Implement parse_output(text) -> (files: dict, deletions: list)
        - Line-based parsing, no regex on content bodies
        - Find all ¤filepath="..."¤ marker lines via regex (marker format only)
        - Pair markers sequentially: open[i] / close[i+1] must have identical path
        - After open marker: scan forward, skip blank lines, find first line starting with ```
        - Before close marker: scan backward, skip blank lines, find last line starting with ```
        - Content = lines strictly between the two fence lines (join with \n)
        - Validate: path match, fence presence, at least one content line
        - Raise ValueError with clear message on any validation failure
        - Parse ¤deletelist¤ section: extract all ¤filepath="..."¤ lines after the marker
        - Return (files, deletions)
    [ ] 'harness/parser.py': Implement apply_output(files, deletions, repo_root)
        - Write each file in `files` to repo_root / path (create parent dirs as needed)
        - Delete each path in `deletions` (ignore if missing, log warning)
        - Return list of applied paths + list of deleted paths for logging
    [ ] 'harness/parser.py': Handle edge cases
        - Tolerate blank lines between marker and fence (skip, don't fail)
        - Tolerate trailing blank line after closing fence
        - Reject if content is empty (fence immediately followed by fence)
        - Reject if a filepath appears as both a write and a delete
        - Reject if the same filepath appears twice in the write section
        - Strip any leading/trailing whitespace on the marker line before matching
    [ ] 'harness/loop.py': Integrate parser into harness loop
        - After receiving LLM response, call parse_output()
        - On success: call apply_output(), log applied/deleted files
        - On ValueError: log error, do NOT apply, return error to user
        - Wrap in try/except so one bad output never corrupts the repo
    [ ] 'harness/test_parser.py': Unit tests
        - Happy path: two files, one deletion, blank lines present
        - Missing closing fence -> ValueError
        - Mismatched open/close path -> ValueError
        - Empty content -> ValueError
        - Duplicate filepath -> ValueError
        - File in both write and delete -> ValueError
        - Content with blank lines internally -> preserved correctly
        - Content with triple backticks inside -> preserved correctly


